# Instance-source upstream revision

What implementing a reviewed instance unit proves wrong in that design or its prototype and placement parents, and what is repaired there. Every selected instance-source export answers for the instance unit it realizes.

## Design revision from instance-source work {#design-revision-from-instance-source-work}

Implementation tests whether the instance design fixes deterministic membership, stable ids, prototypes, transforms, variation, bounds, tiers, and derivation authority. Report any seed, ordering, origin, collision, extent, override, or invalidation rule the export could not implement without choosing locally. Repair the instance or earliest consumed design owner; procedural convenience and iteration order do not authorize population facts.

`@evidence` names every upstream target repaired and the generated or authored case that exposed it. When the export exposes no defect, `@evidenceExclude` names its instance parent and the concrete membership, identities, transforms, variation, and bounds implemented as written. A parentless export or generic deterministic claim covers nothing.

Review question: what did implementing this instance-source export prove wrong in its actual instance or earlier parents, or which concrete parent decisions did it test and find sufficient?

Sources: [OpenUSD PointInstancer on stable ids, prototypes, transforms, and extents](https://openusd.org/release/api/class_usd_geom_point_instancer.html); [NASA on tracing implementation findings to design owners](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/systems-engineering-handbook/)
